Id: fsys020f
Severity: FAIL
Category: filesystem
Check: check_wfile
Fix: chmod o-w the file; if several accounts must write it, give them a group and make it group-writable instead.
References: chmod(1); tigerrc
Controls: CIS 3.3; NIST AC-3; ISO 8.3; CE Secure configuration

A regular file anyone on the system can write, under a directory root
reads programs and configuration from (/etc, /usr, /bin, /sbin, /lib*,
/boot, /root, /opt), a server reads what it serves from (/var/www,
/srv), daemons their queues (/var/spool) or services their state
(/var/lib): the directories Tiger_FSScan_DetailDirs names. Whoever
can write it decides what the program that reads it does; a script, a
configuration file or a rake task is the usual way from one account to
another, or to root. Unlike a world-writable directory, the sticky bit
does not help: the file itself is open. No package ships such a file;
one is the work of an installer or an unpacking with umask 000 (a
vendor's .deb with two gem directories and a gemspec at 777 and 666
was one), or of a log directory a service was given with mode 676.
Elsewhere (home directories, caches, /tmp) such files are counted per
directory instead (fsys021w).
